{
  "skillId": "partial-sum-notation",
  "atomType": "T",
  "theory": {
"intro": "A **sequence** is an ordered list of terms. Formally, a **series** is the sum of the terms of an infinite sequence, written $a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}+\\dots$. The **$n$th partial sum** $S_{n}$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms: $S_{n}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\\dots+a_{n}$.",
"facts": [
"$a_{n}$ (or $T_{n}$) is one term. $S_{n}$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms, for a positive integer $n$.",
"The first partial sum equals the first term: $S_{1}=a_{1}$.",
"Subtracting two partial sums removes their shared first terms. For example, $S_{7}-S_{3}=a_{4}+a_{5}+a_{6}+a_{7}$."
],
"workedExamples": [
{
    "question_text": "A series is $5+12+19+26+33+\\dots$.\nFind $S_{3}$.",
    "solution_text": "$S_{3}=5+12+19$\n$=36$"
  },
{
    "question_text": "A sequence has $n$th term $a_{n}=n^{2}+7$. Find the partial sum $S_{4}$.",
    "solution_text": "Find the first four terms.\n$a_{1}=1^{2}+7=8$\n$a_{2}=2^{2}+7=11$\n$a_{3}=3^{2}+7=16$\n$a_{4}=4^{2}+7=23$\n$S_{4}=8+11+16+23$\n$=58$"
  },
{
    "question_text": "A sequence has $n$th term $a_{n}=n^{2}+2$.\nFind $S_{5}-S_{2}$.",
    "solution_text": "Subtracting removes the first two terms.\n$S_{5}-S_{2}=a_{3}+a_{4}+a_{5}$\n$=(3^{2}+2)+(4^{2}+2)+(5^{2}+2)$\n$=11+18+27$\n$=56$"
  }
]
},
